+# Local callback bridge qualification — not deployment ready
+
+This directory belongs to the talking-head application, not the Chert npm package.
+`handler.ts` implements the package's exported callback type and sends private
+Unix-socket requests to `server.py` / `owner.py`. The Python owner directly reuses
+`receiver/src/chert_reference_agent/avatar_service.py`, `service.py`, `journal.py`,
+and `avatar_adapter.py`. It neither imports the HTTP app nor starts its lifespan,
+Service.start(), warm pool, watcher, or shutdown cleanup. No Chert webhook protocol,
+signature, relay, enrollment, or receiver is reimplemented here.
+
+**Default production admission is intentionally closed.** The deployed classic
+profile requires audio+video, while the CLI requests audio only. Continuous/manual
+slots are process-local and cannot be transferred from the existing Railway
+receiver to this separate process. On-demand preparation must finish inside the
+original four seconds; the existing prepared profile budgets 45 seconds. This
+bridge does not change any of these contracts or retrofit the experimental hook.
+
+## Ownership
+
+A durable operation journal maps operation ID and exact call input digest to one
+application-created room and spec before the existing Service._prepare is called.
+Resource cleanup remains Service._schedule_cleanup → AvatarAdapter.close_room,
+including room absence and owned Muse rendering-room checks. Credentials are
+minted by the existing adapter and returned only in memory over a private socket;
+they are not saved in the operation journal. There are no wrapper SDK effects.
+
+Initialization and health inspect journals/capabilities only. Duplicate input
+coalesces; conflicting input is rejected. A lost response is never retried as a
+new preparation. Recovery of a completed preparation preserves the original room
+until a durable cancellation, authoritative decline/terminal, or expiry.
+Application lifecycle acknowledgments persist event IDs and cancellation intent;
+cleanup is separate and requires the existing owner's verified result. Unknown
+operation cancellation writes a tombstone before reporting clean, fencing a late
+prepare request. Restart never runs the HTTP owner's destructive startup cleanup.
+
+A cancelled SDK request or a crash while `preparing` can leave an effect in flight.
+The bridge reports **unsettled / unknown**, even if a room currently looks absent.
+There is no operation-status API in the existing provider owner that proves such
+an effect cannot arrive later. Do not clear this debt or automatically retry.
+A normal returned preparation handle followed by verified cleanup is locally
+qualified. This is deliberately narrower than arbitrary crash recovery.
+
+## Host requirements and remaining design work
+
+Use a Linux/POSIX host with Python 3.11–3.13 and the receiver's locked dependencies,
+Node 22.23.2 or 24.14.0, single-writer durable local storage, separate 0600 operation
+and resource journals in a 0700 directory, a 0600 socket in a 0700 directory, and
+an immutable compiled handler path/ID. Never share the original receiver's journal
+or namespace ownership across two active processes. Explicit namespace migration
+and old-owner draining/recovery must precede any new host ownership.
+
+The Python process requires application LiveKit credentials; Node needs only
+Chert connection/recovery credentials and `TALKING_HEAD_CALLBACK_SOCKET`.
+`TALKING_HEAD_OPERATION_JOURNAL` selects the additional durable application journal.
+Existing Config.from_env names remain application configuration, including
+CHERT_JOURNAL_PATH and CHERT_WEBHOOK_ROUTES_JSON (required by the reused config;
+use an unused local route, not a new server). No real values are in this directory.
+No process loads `.env` implicitly.
+
+Run `python connector/server.py` only after deployment review; it opens IPC and
+journals, not agents. An existing stale socket is a fail-closed restart condition;
+supervision must prove the prior writer is dead before removing only that socket.
+Never remove journals or Chert state to get past startup checks.
+
+Before a live pilot, the application needs a reviewed compatible media policy,
+a durable way to reserve an already-ready slot from its actual owner (without
+startup/health warming), and owner-driven expiry/cleanup supervision independent
+of the CLI process. The bridge's prototype server intentionally does not start
+Service._watch, whose recovery/pruning assumes the HTTP owner's journals. The
+existing remote agent's failsafe is not evidence of provider cleanup. A provider
+operation fence/status receipt is needed for automatic recovery of interrupted
+preparation. These are substantive application-integration gates, not CLI polish.
+
+## Isolated tests
+
+Tests replace only provider transports used by the real AvatarAdapter. No HTTP
+receiver, RTC connection, agent worker, warming process or external provider runs.
+`fixture_server.py` refuses Internet socket connections and uses synthetic keys.
+`--synthetic-audio-policy` exists only in this test fixture: it exercises callback
+mechanics with fast fake readiness, while retaining the real adapter's audio AND
+video checks. It is not a supported production profile and is never read by the
+production server. Without it, tests verify the real profile declines.
+
+The installed npm tests in the CLI repository compile this actual handler against
+the installed tarball types and run it through CLI HTTP/WebSocket transport and
+Python IPC. See `chert-facetime-cli/docs/byor-qualification/RESULTS.md` for exact
+results, limits and deployment proposal. Do not substitute fixture_server.py for
+server.py in supervision or ship the synthetic provider as an application backend.
